 { Vadim Zvjaginsev - Rodrigo Vasquez, World Rapid Ch., Khanty-Mansiysk 2013 Learning objective: The central break d4-d5 can be very powerful if Black does not effectively prepare against it. } 1. c4 c6 2. e4 d5 3. exd5 Nf6 4. Nc3 cxd5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Nf3 e6 7. Bc4 Nc6 8. O-O Be7 9. d4 O-O 10. Re1 Nf6 ( { The alternative } 10... Bf6 $5 { certainly deserves attention. Black preserves the blockade on d5 , preventing possible d4-d5 breaks, and after } 11. Ne4 { simply } Be7 { . } ) 
11. a3 { A standard move, preventing Nb4-d5 and clearing the a2-square for the bishop. } ( { PH: It's important to add that } 11. Bg5 { is typically answered by } h6 12. Bh4 Nh5 { , forcing the exchange of bishops, } 13. Bxe7 Nxe7 { , and Black is quite okay. White doesn't usually put the bishop on g5 in a position like this voluntarily. } ) 
a6 $6 { This is somewhat slow, especially since ...b7-b5 will greatly boost the strength of the break d4-d5. } ( { More sensible was } 11... b6 $5 { and developing the bishop next move. } 12. Qd3 ( { Now the break } 11... a6 11. d5 { is not as effective, in view of } Na5 13. Ba2 Nxd5 14. Nxd5 exd5 15. Qxd5 Be6 { when Black was very close to equality in Durarbeyli - Mirzoev, Baku 2009 } { _ } ) 
Bb7 { with a standard isolani position. } ) 
12. Ba2 { This position has been reached over one hundred times on my database. And almost fifty players chose: } b5 $2 { A very serious mistake. } ( { Black could have obtained a playable position after } 12... Qc7 $5 13. Be3 Rd8 14. Qc2 $14 { though White retains the initiative, and may proceed with Rad1 followed by d4-d5. } ) 
13. d5 $1 { Perfect timing! Black's development is insufficient to parry all of White's threats. } exd5 14. Nxd5 Nxd5 ( { PH: } 14... Ra7 { would still be decent for Black: } 15. Be3 Rd7 16. Nxe7 Nxe7 $14 { White has gained the bishop pair, but with good play Black should be able to make a draw. } ) 
15. Qxd5 Bb7 ( { Of course not: } 15... Qxd5 $2 16. Bxd5 Bb7 17. Bxc6 Bxc6 18. Rxe7 $18 ) 
16. Qh5 $16 { Black finds himself in a very difficult position, as White is bringing his pieces into a kingside attack and there is no time to organize a defence. } Qc7 ( 16... h6 $2 { loses by force after: } 17. Bxh6 $1 gxh6 18. Qg6 Kh8 19. Qxh6 Kg8 20. Bb1 f5 21. Ba2 Rf7 22. Qg6 ) 
 ( 16... Qd6 $6 { This only aggravates Black's problems, because of: } 17. Bg5 { Threatening Rad1. } Rad8 ( { If } 17... Bxg5 $2 18. Nxg5 Qg6 19. Qxg6 hxg6 20. Nxf7 Rxf7 21. Rad1 { and Black is defenceless, for example: } Raf8 22. Rd7 Bc8 23. Rxf7 Rxf7 24. Re8 ) 
18. Bxe7 Nxe7 19. Ng5 Qg6 20. Qxg6 $1 ( 19... Qc7 19. Qxh7 Qxh7 21. Nxh7 Kxh7 22. Rxe7 Bd5 { gives Black drawing chances in the double-rook ending. } ) 
Nxg6 21. Nxf7 Rxf7 22. Rad1 $1 Rxd1 23. Rxd1 Bc6 24. Rd6 Be8 25. Rxa6 { White had a winning endgame in Gurgenidze - Anikaev, Kislovodsk 1972. } ) 
 ( 16... g6 17. Qh6 Nd4 18. Ng5 Bxg5 19. Bxg5 Qd6 20. Rad1 $16 { White has a very dangerous dynamic advantage, due to the pair of bishops and the exposed black king; for example } Rae8 $2 { loses immediately to } 21. Bxf7 $1 ) 
17. Bg5 $1 { The most precise continuation. } ( 17. Ng5 $5 { deserves attention. } Bxg5 18. Bxg5 Rae8 19. Bd2 $16 { with a big advantage due to the bishop pair and superior piece coordination. } ) 
Bxg5 ( { If } 17... Rae8 $2 { Black loses at once, in view of: } 18. Bxe7 Rxe7 19. Rxe7 Qxe7 20. Re1 Qf6 21. Ng5 Qg6 22. Qxg6 hxg6 23. Nxf7 $1 { Black cannot recapture on f7 because 24. Re8+ wins the rook. } ) 
 ( { If } 17... Bd6 { the rook transfer decides the game after: } 18. Re4 Ne5 19. Rh4 h6 20. Nxe5 Bxe5 21. Bxh6 $18 ) 
18. Nxg5 h6 19. Re3 Na5 $2 { The final mistake in a very difficult position. } ( { The only defence was } 19... Ne5 { preventing } 20. Qg6 { , but after } 20. Ne6 $1 fxe6 21. Rxe5 $16 { White will be a full pawn up. } ) 
20. Qg6 $1 { +- Nice finish! } hxg5 21. Rh3 Rfc8 ( 21... Rfe8 { does not help because of: } 22. Rh7 $1 ( { but not } 21... Rfc8 21. Qh7 $2 Kf8 { when the king escapes } ) 
Qe5 ( { or } 22... Kf8 23. Qxg7 Ke7 24. Qxf7 Kd6 25. Qxc7 ) 
23. Qxf7 Kxh7 24. Qh5 ) 
22. Re1 { Checkmate is unavoidable. Final Remarks: 1. Black's position would have been much safer had he kept his knight on d5. 2. It is absolutely necessary to prevent, or at least prepare against, a possible d4-d5 break. 3. Black's biggest mistake was playing the weakening moves ...a7-a6 and ...b7-b5, wasting time and making the d4-d5 break even stronger. 4. After d4-d5 was played, the position was nearly lost and extremely difficult to conduct. } 
 1-0

