Bxc5 11. e3 a6 ( { PH: The computer gives an even stronger continuation: } 11... Qe7 12. Be2 Ne5 13. O-O Bg4 $15 { , quickly mounting some pressure on White's kingside } ) 
12. Be2 b5 13. O-O Bb7 14. Rfd1 ( { PH: Here White should have realized that Black's position is perfectly fine, and instead try to liquidate the position with } 14. b4 Bd6 15. a4 { , e.g. } bxa4 16. Nxa4 Rc8 17. Qb3 Ne4 18. Bc3 Qe7 $11 { likely the remaining queenside pawns are soon exchanged. } ) 
Rc8 15. Be1 Re8 16. Rac1 Nf8 { Black's knight is heading toward e6, in order to support the break ...d5-d4. } ( { Somewhat stronger was } 16... Nb6 $5 { heading to the c4-square. } 17. Nd4 Nc4 18. Bf3 Qe7 19. h3 Qe5 $15 { Black is slightly better as the isolated d5-pawn is well supported, and he may create pressure on the centre and kingside with ...Bd6 and ...Ne4. } ) 
17. Nd4 ( { PH: Here again } 17. b4 $1 { was necessary, trying to liquidate the queenside: } Bd6 18. a4 $1 Bxb4 19. axb5 Bxc3 20. Bxc3 Ne4 21. Qb2 Nxc3 22. Rxc3 Rxc3 23. Qxc3 axb5 24. Bxb5 Re7 { and at the end of the line White is a little bit better, but Black should easily be able to defend this. } ) 
Qb6 18. Bf3 $6 { It was necessary to support the d4-square. } ( { Better was } 18. Qd2 Ne6 19. Nxe6 Qxe6 ( { or } 19. Bf3 fxe6 $5 ) 
20. Bf3 Bb6 { with a standard isolani position. } ( 20. Bf3 Red8 $11 { The d5-pawn is immune to } ) 
21. Nxd5 $2 { in view of } Rxc1 22. Nxf6 Qxf6 23. Rxc1 Bxf3 24. gxf3 Qxf3 $17 { when Black regains the pawn favourably. } ) 
Bxd4 $1 19. Rxd4 ( 19. exd4 $2 { does not help, as } Ne6 20. Qd3 Nxd4 $1 { wins a pawn, since } 21. Qxd4 $4 { loses to } Rxe1 $1 22. Rxe1 Qxd4 ) 
Ne6 20. Rd2 $6 ( { The option } 20. Rdd1 $6 { does not solve White's problems either. } d4 $1 21. Bxb7 Qxb7 22. exd4 Nf4 23. f3 N6d5 { and Black is threatening ...Nxc3 followed by ...Ne2 or ...Re2. White is in deep trouble, for example: } 24. Rd2 a5 25. Qb1 Nxc3 26. Rxc3 Rxc3 27. bxc3 Ne2 28. Kh1 Nxc3 29. Qa1 b4 $19 { The passed b-pawn, together with tactical threats on the first rank, make Black's initiative irresistible. } ) 
 ( { Instead } 20. Rh4 $1 { (as suggested by Krasenkow) allows White to stay in the game: } Rc4 ( { The break } 20. Rd2 d4 $6 { is no longer effective, since after } 21. Bxb7 Qxb7 22. exd4 { the squares e4 and f4 are not available to Black's knights. } ) 
 ( { On the other hand } 20. Rd2 Red8 $5 { ⩱ is a worthy alternative, preparing ...d5-d4 and taking advantage of the misplaced rook on h4. } ) 
21. Rxc4 dxc4 $15 { Black has some advantage owing to his superior piece coordination. } ) 
d4 $1 { Aronian decides to sacrifice his isolated pawn, activating all of his pieces; Black seizes an overwhelming initiative. } 21. Bxb7 ( { Much worse is } 21. exd4 $2 Bxf3 22. gxf3 Nxd4 23. Qd1 ( { or } 23. Qd3 Nb3 $19 ) 
Rxe1 $1 { -+ } ) 
Qxb7 ( { Of course not } 21... dxc3 $2 { due to: } 22. Bxc8 cxd2 23. Bxd2 $18 ) 
22. exd4 { Black is a pawn down, but now all his pieces collaborate in the attack. } Ne4 $1 23. Rdd1 ( 23. Qxe4 $2 Qxe4 24. Nxe4 Rxc1 { is also hopeless. } ) 
 ( { But a better defence was } 23. f3 Nxd2 24. Qxd2 { , though Black has a big advantage. } ) 
Nf4 $1 { It is possible that Huzman allowed the ...d5-d4 sacrifice because he did not expect Black's knights to be so strong. Black is immediately threatening ...Nxc3, followed by ...Qxg2#. } 24. d5 { Blocking the a8-h1diagonal. } ( { Black wins an exchange and the game after } 24. f3 Nxc3 25. Bxc3 Ne2 26. Kh1 Nxc1 27. Rxc1 a5 $1 { followed by ...b5-b4. } ) 
a5 $1 { This strong moves enables a quick win. } ( { Instead the tempting } 24... Nxc3 $6 { is less precise, since after } 25. Bxc3 Ne2 26. Kh1 Nxc1 27. Qxc1 $17 { White's d5 pawn allows him to hold for longer. } ) 
25. Qb3 b4 26. axb4 axb4 { Threatening ...Nc5, followed by ...bxc3. } 27. Na2 ( 27. Nxe4 { does not help due to: } Rxc1 28. Rxc1 Ne2 29. Kh1 Nxc1 $19 ) 
Ne2+ 28. Kh1 Nxc1 { Black wins the exchange and the rest is easy. } 29. Nxc1 Nf6 30. d6 ( { The try } 30. Bxb4 { is refuted by } Rb8 31. Na2 Nxd5 $1 32. Qxd5 ( { or } 32. Rxd5 $2 Re1 ) 
Qxd5 33. Rxd5 Rxb4 $1 { since } 34. Nxb4 $2 Re1 { is mate. } ) 
Qc6 31. Na2 Qxd6 $1 32. Bxb4 ( 32. Rxd6 $4 Rxe1 ) 
Qb6 33. f3 Qf2 { Final Remarks: 1. Time is essential to obtain a good piece set-up. White's imprecision with 10. a3 allowed Black to fight for an advantage from the start. 2. White's biggest mistake was not to reinforce his control of d4 with 18. Qd2, in which case the position would have been close to equal. 3. The ...d5-d4 pawn sacrifice gave Black an essentially winning initiative. } 
