 { Radoslaw Wojtaszek - Laurent Fressinet, Wijk aan Zee 2011 Learning objectives: 1. The side which possesses an isolani in the endgame is limited to passive defence. 2. This game exemplifies how to make progress in such a situation. As we can readily observe, White has obtained a dream position against the isolani, as most of the pieces are off the board, and now he can play a pleasant, superior endgame. } 1... Rd7 2. g4 $1 { Claiming space on the kingside, and threatening g4-g5 followed by Nxd5. } h6 3. f3 { Taking the e4-square away from Black's knight. } Rad8 4. Rcd1 { Blacks pieces are tied down. } g6 5. Kf2 { Improving slowly, while Black has nothing better than passive defence. } Kg7 6. h4 $1 { Threatening g4-g5, and inviting Black to weaken his position with ...g6-g5. } ( { An interesting alternative is } 6. Ne2 $5 ( { However, after } 6. Ne2 { Black can play } Re7 7. Nc3 Rde8 $1 { , causing some problems for White by pressuring the e3-pawn. } ) 
 { since the passive } Kf8 7. Nc3 { runs into } Kg7 8. h4 g5 9. h5 $16 { when the h6-pawn is very weak. } ) 
g5 { This is more or less forced. Black cannot really proceed without playing ...g6-g5, for example: } ( 6... Rc8 7. R3d2 ( { The premature } 6... g5 6. g5 $2 { would allow Black to solve his problems with: } Rc2 8. R3d2 Rxd2 9. Rxd2 hxg5 10. hxg5 Nh7 11. Nxd5 Nxg5 $11 ) 
Rcd8 ( 7... Rc5 $2 { loses a pawn after: } 8. e4 $16 ) 
8. Ne2 $5 { Heading toward c3 before the decisive g4-g5. } ( 7... g5 7. g5 $2 { is again premature, due to } hxg5 9. hxg5 Nh7 ) 
a6 9. Nc3 Kf8 ( { If Black now changes his mind and plays } 9... g5 { then } 10. h5 $1 { ± turns the h6-pawn into a serious weakness. } ) 
10. Na4 Kg7 11. g5 hxg5 12. hxg5 Nh7 13. Nc5 ( 12... g5 12. f4 $5 { ± } ) 
Rc7 ( 13... Rd6 $2 14. Nxb7 $18 ) 
14. Rxd5 $16 { White has a healthy extra pawn. } ) 
