 { Oleg Korneev - Rustam Kasimdzhanov, Melilla 2011 Learning objectives: 1. This example reinforces the idea that having an isolani in the endgame is a big problem. 2. Black can use the weakness of the isolani to force the creation of new queenside weaknesses. } 1... c6 { White's main difficulty in this position is his lack of material denies him hope of counterplay. } 2. b4 { This move is necessary sooner or later in order to prevent ...c6-c5. } ( { If White does not play b2-b4, then after } 2. Kh2 Qd8 3. Qb4 Qd7 4. Qc4 Rd5 { the threat of ...c6-c5 forces White to play } 5. b4 { after all. } ) 
Qd8 3. a3 h5 { Black improves his position as much as possible before going for decisive action. } 4. Qf4 g6 5. Qe5 Rd5 6. Qe4 Kg7 { Black brings the king closer to the centre, preparing himself for a rook endgame. } 7. Kg1 a5 $1 { A typical break. Now that everything is ready, Black creates a new target in White's camp. White must choose between having a weak pawn on b4, or allowing ...c6-c5. } ( { PH: A slower approach was also interesting: } 7... b6 $5 { intending ...c5, but here White gets some counterplay: } 8. Qe2 c5 9. bxc5 bxc5 10. Qxa6 Rxd4 11. Re1 c4 $15 { The c-pawn is strong and White's king is a bit more exposed, but the exchanges have overall been helpful for White. } ) 
8. Kh1 $6 { Making things easier for Black, though the alternatives were not objectively better. } ( { More stubborn was: } 8. bxa5 { But Black has good winning chances after: } c5 $1 ( { White has better prospects after } 8. Kh1 Qxa5 9. Rd3 Qd8 { threatening ...c6-c5, and now: } 10. Rb3 $1 b5 11. Rb4 $15 ) 
9. Rd2 ( { Searching for counterplay with } 9. Rb1 cxd4 10. Rxb7 { just barely fails against } d3 11. Qe6 d2 ( { But not } 11. Kh1 Rf5 $2 12. Qd7 $11 ) 
12. Qxf7 Kh6 $17 { and Black is nearly winning, but I will not include any more lines as they are extremely long and quite irrelevant. } ) 
 ( { PH: Here analogous with the line after 7...b6, perhaps best was } 9. Qe2 Rxd4 10. Re1 c4 $15 { . } ) 
Qd7 { Slowly preparing ...Rxd4. } ( { If } 9. Kh1 cxd4 $6 10. Rd3 { White has chances of holding. } ) 
 { Hence White goes for: } 10. Re2 cxd4 11. Rd2 d3 $17 { Although White's defence is very difficult to say the least. } ) 
axb4 9. axb4 Qd6 $17 { The b4-pawn is basically lost already. } 10. Qb1 Rb5 11. Qa1 Kh7 ( { More accurate was } 11... f6 $1 { , heading for a queen endgame: } 12. Rb1 Rxb4 13. Rxb4 Qxb4 14. Qa8 Qe1 15. Kh2 Qe7 16. Kh1 ( { Worse is } 15... Kh7 15. Qc8 $2 Qd6 17. Kh1 b5 $19 ) 
Qd7 $17 { Black should win by pushing his b-pawn, though it requires good technique. } ) 
12. Qa2 Kg8 13. Qa8+ Qf8 $6 { Black heads into a favourable rook endgame, but misses a stronger option. } ( 13... Kg7 14. Qa1 f6 $1 { would transpose into the variation 11... f6. } ) 
14. Qxf8+ Kxf8 15. Rb1 Ke7 16. Kg1 Kd6 17. Kf2 Kd5 ( { PH: Here } 17... h4 $1 { and only then the same maneuver would have been a more convincing win. See the note to 19.Ke4 for a resource that Black prevents with ...h4. } ) 
18. Ke3 Kc4 { Finally, the b4-pawn is lost. } 19. Ke4 $2 ( { White misses a great defensive resource in } 19. Rc1 $1 Kxb4 20. h4 { The game is far from over, for example: } Ka5 $5 21. Ra1 Kb6 22. Ra8 Rd5 $17 { White has a difficult defence ahead of him, but all is not yet lost. PH: indeed, } 23. g4 $1 { would create great winning difficulties for Black. } ) 
f5+ $1 { Keeping control of the game. } ( { If } 19... Rxb4 { then } 20. Rc1 $1 Kb5 21. Ke5 { , heading to f6 in order to capture Black's kingside pawns. } ) 
20. Kf4 Rxb4 21. Rc1+ Kxd4 $19 { The rest is easy but instructive. } 22. Rd1+ Kc3+ 23. Kg5 Rd4 24. Rb1 Rd6 $1 { This prevents any counterplay associated with Kxg6, and wins the game nicely. } 25. Rb6 ( 25. Rxb7 { does not help: } c5 { will be followed by ...c4, ...Kd2, ...c3-c2 winning. } ) 
Kd4 26. g4 hxg4 27. hxg4 fxg4 28. fxg4 Kd5 29. Rxb7 c5 30. Kf4 c4 31. Ke3 c3 { Right on time! White can do nothing to stop the pawn. } 32. Rc7 ( 32. Kd3 $2 { loses to } Kc6 ) 
Rc6 $1 33. Rd7+ ( { The pawn endgame is lost after } 33. Rxc6 Kxc6 34. Kd3 Kd5 35. Kxc3 Ke4 ) 
Ke5 34. Rd1 c2 35. Rc1 g5 36. Kd2 Kf4 37. Rf1+ ( 37. Rg1 c1=Q $1 { and wins. } ) 
Kxg4 38. Kc1 Rc5 $1 { White cannot prevent the promotion of the g-pawn. Final remarks 1. The standard plan ...Qd8 and ...Rd5 forces White to play b2-b4, in order to prevent ...c6-c5 winning a pawn. 2. Once b2-b4 had been played, the break ...a7-a5 forces the creation of a weak b4-pawn, or allows Black to finally achieve ...c6-c5. 3. The initial position looks like just a very slight advantage for Black, but in fact White lost the game almost without making a further mistake. 4. It should be noted that this version of the isolani is slightly different from previous examples, as Black had a pawn on the c-file, rather than the e-file. } 
 0-1

