 { Pavel Simacek - Baadur Jobava, Legnica 2013 Learning objective: This game illustrates how dangerous the Black isolani can be when White lacks his e-pawn. } 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 c5 4. Ngf3 a6 5. exd5 exd5 6. dxc5 Bxc5 { We have reached a far less common version of the isolani. White usually has a pawn on the e-file, instead of the c-pawn. Many of the typical plans are still the same, but the lack of White's e-pawn makes his king far more vulnerable, particularly on the a7-g1 diagonal. } 7. Nb3 Bb6 8. Be2 $6 { This development is rather imprecise, since it blocks the e-file, thus preventing a future Re1 followed by Be3, which would neutralize the action of the bishop on b6. } ( { Correct was } 8. Bd3 Ne7 ( { PH: If } 8. Be2 Nf6 9. Qe2 $1 { every way in which Black can interpose a piece on the e-file has clear downsides. In fact it turns out that } Qe7 $1 { is the best reply by Black. } ) 
9. O-O Bg4 10. Re1 h6 11. h3 Bh5 12. c3 Nbc6 13. Be3 $14 { Karpov - Vaganian, Skopje 1976. } ) 
 ( 8. Qe2 $5 { inviting an early queen trade, also deserves attention. } ) 
Nf6 9. O-O h6 ( { PH: } 9... O-O $5 { seems fine. It invites the pin with } 10. Bg5 { , but after } h6 11. Bxf6 Qxf6 12. Qxd5 Nc6 { Black has so much compensation that he is just better. } ) 
10. Bf4 Nc6 11. c3 O-O 12. Qc2 Re8 $15 { Black has developed his pieces naturally, and has a very pleasant position. } 13. h3 $2 { This is a serious mistake, wasting a move and weakening the kingside. White probably wanted to prevent ...Bg4, but there are much bigger problems to worry about, as we will see. } ( { It was necessary to reorganize the pieces with } 13. Bd3 Ne4 ( 13. h3 Bg4 14. Nfd4 $1 { - piece trades do not benefit Black } ) 
14. Rae1 Qf6 15. Be3 { though after } Bc7 $15 { Black has a comfortable position. } ) 
Ne4 $17 { Black intends to follow up with ...Qf6 building a strong kingside initiative. White already faces severe problems such as the strong pressure on the a7-g1 diagonal. } 14. Nbd4 { Hoping to block Black's strong bishop. } ( { The natural } 14. Rad1 { fails to } Qf6 15. Bc1 Bxh3 $1 { winning due to the hanging queen on c2: } 16. gxh3 Qg6 17. Kh2 Bc7 18. Kh1 Nxf2 $1 19. Rxf2 Qxc2 ) 
 ( 14. Bh2 Qf6 $17 { leaves White under definite pressure; one threat would be } Bxh3 { . Notice how strong the black bishop on b6 is. } ) 
Qf6 15. Be3 { White's seemingly solid position will fall apart after Black's strong reply. } ( { Black is simply a pawn up after: } 15. Qc1 Nxd4 16. cxd4 Bxd4 17. Nxd4 Qxd4 $17 ) 
Bxh3 $19 { This typical tactical blow exemplifies how quickly Black's attack can develop in this version of the isolani. White's pieces cannot cope with so much pressure. } 16. gxh3 $6 { Making things easier. } ( { Better was } 16. Qb3 Nxd4 17. cxd4 Bg4 { , though Black is a clear pawn up. } ) 
Qg6+ 17. Kh2 ( 17. Kh1 $2 Nxd4 18. Nxd4 Bxd4 $1 { Black regains the piece, since White's queen is hanging: } 19. Bxd4 $2 Nxf2 $1 20. Rxf2 Qxc2 $19 ) 
Bc7+ 18. Kh1 Nxd4 19. Nxd4 Qd6 $1 { Threatening checkmate on h2 and forcing the knight to abandon the d4-square, this leaving the queen on c2 defenceless again. } 20. Nf3 ( { No better is } 20. f4 Ng3 ) 
Qe6 { White resigned, since material loss is unavoidable. } ( 20... Qe6 20. Ng1 ( 20... Qe6 20. Kg2 Qg6 { transposing to 21. Ng1, after 23...Qg6+ } ) 
Qe5 $1 22. Nf3 ( 21... Qe6 21. f4 Ng3 $19 ) 
Qf5 23. Kg2 Qg6 24. Ng5 ( 23... Qe6 23. Kh1 $2 Ng3 25. fxg3 Qxc2 $19 ) 
Bd8  { followed by ...Bxg5 winning easily. } ) 
 { Final remarks 1. White should have neutralized Black's bishop on b6 with Re1 and Be3, as mentioned in the note to move 8. 2. Black's initiative develops so easily that one may wonder if this version of the isolani is simply inferior for White. This is not necessarily true, though White requires extra care and imprecision prove more costly, mainly because the king is more vulnerable. } 
 0-1

