[Result "1-0"]

 { Nikita Vitiugov - Viktor Bologan, Eilat 2012 Learning objectives: 1. This game illustrates how important it is to have the good bishop when you have an isolani. 2. Without this bishop it is extremely difficult to create counterplay even in the middlegame. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 b6 7. Bg5 Bb7 8. e3 d6 9. Ne2 Nbd7 10. Qc2 c5 11. Rd1 Qe7 12. Nc3 cxd4 13. Rxd4 h6 14. Bh4 Rfd8 15. f3 { White intends to proceed with Be2, O-O, and Rfd1, possibly followed by e3-e4, obtaining a favourable version of the typical Hedgehog structure, which is studied in detail later in this book. } d5 16. cxd5 exd5 17. Bf2 { We have reached the position of interest. Based on the previous games we might think there is generally enough counterplay to compensate for the isolated d5-pawn, but this game proves the opposite. The key factor is the lack of Black's dark-squared bishop, which is often associated with threats along the b8-h2 diagonal. In this game, Black finds himself unable to produce sufficient counterplay, and must passively wait to suffer a painful defeat. } Nc5 18. Be2 Ne6 19. Rd2 a6 ( { Getting rid of the d5 weakness with } 19... d4 $6 { will not give Black enough counterplay after: } 20. exd4 Nf4 21. O-O N6d5 22. Re1 Qg5 23. Bf1 Nh3 24. Kh1 $14 ) 
20. O-O b5 21. Rfd1 $14 { White's advantage is quite obvious. The ...d5-d4 break is impossible and Black is tied to the defence of the weak d5-pawn. } Rac8 22. Qb1 Nc5 23. Bf1 { White frees the e2-square to rearrange his pieces with Ne2-d4, controlling many key squares. } Nb3 24. Rc2 Na5 { The previous knight moves make good sense. Since the e4-square has been taken away with f2-f3, Black's natural reaction is to place a knight on c4, pressuring e3. } 25. Ne2 ( 25. a4 $5 { was also possible, pressuring Black's queenside. } bxa4 ( { Or } 25. Ne2 b4 26. Ne2 Rxc2 27. Qxc2 Rc8 28. Qb1 Nc4 29. Nd4 $16 { when there is little counterplay and the weaknesses on a6, b4 and d5 are hard to deal with. } ) 
26. Nxa4 Rxc2 27. Qxc2 Rc8 28. Qd3 Qb4 29. Qd4 $16 { Black's position is quite delicate. } ) 
Rxc2 ( { The try } 25... Nc4 { is met by } 26. Qc1 $14 { which is similar to the game. } ) 
26. Qxc2 Rc8 27. Qb1 Nc4 28. Nd4 { White's pieces work together in harmony, while Black's pieces lack ways to improve. } Qe5 ( 28... Nxe3 $2 { fails to } 29. Re1 ) 
 ( { An alternative is } 28... Nd7 { but White is better after } 29. Rc1 Ndb6 30. Rc2 $1 { ⩲ followed by Qc1 preparing b2-b3, which is analogous to the game. } ) 
29. Rd3 Nd7 30. Rc3 { White prepares the invasion along the c-file, and Black does not have a satisfactory reply. } Re8 $6 ( { It was preferable to fight for the c-file with: } 30... Ndb6 { Although after } 31. Qc1 Qe7 32. b3 { Black's position is difficult to hold, for example: } Ne5 ( 32... Nxa3 $6 33. Rxc8 Nxc8 ( 33... Bxc8 $2 34. Qc6 Qd8 35. Qc5 { and the knight on a3 is lost. } ) 
34. Nf5 { And White regains the pawn favourably after: } Qd7 ( 34... Qf8 $2 { runs into a nice tactical refutation: } 35. Qc7 $1 Ba8 36. Qxc8 ) 
35. Nxh6 gxh6 36. Qxa3 $16 ) 
33. Be1 Qd7 34. Rxc8 Nxc8 35. Bb4 $16 ) 
31. Qc1 { Defending the a3-pawn and preparing the invasion with b2-b3 followed by Rc7. } Nf8 32. b3 Nb6 33. Bg3 Qf6 34. Rc7 Ba8 $2 { This makes things easier. } ( 34... Rc8 { was the last attempt to fight, but after } 35. Qc5 h5 36. Qe7 $1 Qxe7 37. Rxe7 { Black's position is very close to lost, for example } Nfd7 $2 38. a4 $1 { followed by a4-a5 winning a piece. } ) 
35. Ra7 $18 { Black's position collapses; both the a6- and b5-pawns will be lost. } Rc8 36. Rc7 Re8 37. Ra7 Rc8 38. Qd2 Nbd7 39. Qa5 { Necessary to capture the a6- and b5-pawns. } Rc1 ( 39... Nc5 { does not help due to: } 40. b4 Ncd7 41. Rxa6 $18 ) 
40. Rxa8 { White is a whole piece up, and the rest is easy. } Qg6 41. e4 dxe4 42. Qd2 Ra1 43. Nc2 Rxf1+ 44. Kxf1 Qc6 45. Rd8 exf3 46. gxf3 Qxf3+ 47. Kg1 Qc6 48. Ne3 Qb6 49. Qxd7 Qxe3+ 50. Bf2 Qc1+ 51. Kg2 Qxa3 52. Qd6 { Final remarks 1. Black's biggest strategic mistake was accepting an isolani without having his good bishop. It is likely that Bologan did not properly assess how little counterplay he would have. 2. White's piece arrangement with pawns on e3/f3, Bf1, Bf2 and Nd4 was ideal. It covered most essential squares while maintaining a solid and dynamic position. 3. Black did not have a good response against the plan Rc3, Qc1 and b2-b3 followed by penetrating down the c-file. Therefore, Black's position was lost in practice ever since move 30. This means a defence was only possible assuming near-perfect play, which even a top-player like Bologan was unable to deliver. } 
 1-0

