 { Magnus Carlsen - Wang Yue, Linares 2009 Learning objective: In contrast to the previous game, the player who has an isolani can obtain good counterplay if he possesses his good bishop. This is true despite already having traded two minor pieces. } 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 a6 5. Nf3 b5 6. b3 Bg4 7. Bd2 Nbd7 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 b4 10. Na4 e5 $1 { Black correctly converts the game into an isolani battle where he will have enough counterplay. Notice how far White's knight is from the ideal d4-square. } ( { Instead } 10... e6 11. Bd3 a5 12. Rc1 Bd6 13. O-O O-O 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Rc6 $14 { gives White a pleasingly dominating position with little black counterplay. } ) 
11. Rc1 Bd6 ( { PH: It looks tempting to play } 11... e4 $2 { , getting a French structure without a bad bishop, but after } 12. Qd1 Be7 13. cxd5 cxd5 14. Qc2 $16 { White is just much better thanks to his control over the c-file (there is a threat of Qc7 and exchanging queens) and easy development. } ) 
12. cxd5 cxd5 13. dxe5 Nxe5 14. Qd1 O-O 15. Be2 a5 { We have reached the position of interest, where White has the pair of bishops and a healthy pawn structure. Black needs to arrange his pieces well in order to achieve good counterplay. } 16. Rc2 Qe7 17. Bc1 { By following up with Bb2, White wants to improve his bishop, as it was useless on d2. } ( { Castling first with } 17. O-O Rad8 { is likely to transpose to the game, as White will have to play Bc1 sooner or later. } ) 
Rad8 18. Bb2 Ng6 19. O-O Ne4 20. Bd4 { This blockades the d5-pawn and prepares Bb6 pressuring Black's queenside. } Nh4 { Black's knights are ideally placed on e4 and h4, pressuring the kingside and inviting White to weaken his position. Black has full compensation for his isolated pawn. } 21. Bd3 ( { If White simply intends to grab some material with: } 21. Bb6 { Black can give up an exchange for activity with: } Bb8 $5 ( 21. Bd3 Qg5 $5 { also deserves attention. } ) 
22. Bxd8 ( { White must accept the challenge, otherwise ...Rd6 will follow: } 22. Kh1 $2 Rd6 $17 { with a dangerous attack. } ) 
Rxd8 { Here Black has enough threats to compensate for the exchange sacrifice, say after: } 23. Bd3 ( { Or } 23. Qc1 Qd6 24. f4 Ba7 $1 { and White is tied up: } 25. Bd3 ( 25. Rc8 $2 Bxe3 $1 { -+ } ) 
Nf5 26. Rf3 Nxe3 27. Rxe3 Qxf4 28. Rce2 f5 $1 { This curious position is dynamically balanced, for example } 29. Bxe4 fxe4 30. Kh1 Rf8 31. Qe1 Rc8 $13 { when it is unclear how White may release his pieces. } ) 
Qd6 24. f4 ( { But not } 24. g3 $2 Nxg3 $1 { ∓ } ) 
Nf5 25. Bxe4 Nxe3 26. Bxh7 Kxh7 27. Qd3 Qg6 28. Qxg6 fxg6 $11 ) 
Nf5 { Black wants to get rid of White's strong bishop on d4. } ( { Worthy of consideration is } 21... Bb8 $5 { with kingside threats, such as ...Qd6 or ...f7-f5-f4, e.g. } 22. Re1 f5 23. f4 g5 $1 { ⇆ with a dangerous kingside initiative. } ) 
