 { Final Comment: Isolani on the Fifth Rank The position in the diagram below occurred in Narciso Dublan - Gonzales Vidal, Badalona 2005. In this position Black continued with the strong ...d4. } 11... d4 $1 { Now the game takes a somewhat different character. The reader should take a moment to analyze this position, and come to realize Black's position is the more comfortable already, since his isolated pawn is by no means vulnerable on the d4-square, while White's forces are severely constrained. Black will simply proceed by developing his pieces and exerting pressure down the e-file. } ( { The alternative } 11... Ne4 12. Ncd4 Bc5 13. Be3 O-O 14. Rc1 Nxd4 15. Bxd4 { gives White a comfortable position with favourable long-term chances since Black's good bishop will be traded. } ) 
12. Nce1 { Heading towards d3; it is necessary to prevent the d4-pawn from advancing further with a potential ...d4-d3 break, which would unleash the potential of Black's better-placed minor pieces. } ( { The careless } 12. b3 Bc5 13. Bb2 O-O 14. Qd3 { is refuted by } Bd5 $1 15. Rad1 Be4 16. Qd2 d3 $1 { opening up the position with winning threats: } 17. exd3 Bxd3 18. Bxf6 ( { Or } 18. Rfe1 $2 Ne4 ) 
gxf6 19. Rfe1 Nb4 $1 20. Nxb4 Bxb4 21. Qf4 Bxe1 $19 ) 
 ( { PH: The computer shows its own unique understanding and gives } 12. b4 $1 { , intending to answer } Nxb4 { with } 13. Bg5 $1 { when Black has difficulties dealing with both Rb1 and Bxf6, while of Black doesn't take the pawn, a4 and b5 is attractive. } ) 
 ( { PH: A more human try is } 12. a4 $5 a5 13. Qd3 { when the queen will come to b5 under favorable circumstances. } ) 
Be7 13. Nd3 { Unlike the usual knight on d4, which blocks the isolani and controls the board, this knight on d3 is rather passive. } O-O 14. Bg5 Rfe8 $15 { Black mobilizes his pieces with ease, while the d4-pawn constrains White's forces. } 15. Bxf6 ( { An alternative was } 15. Re1 h6 16. Bxf6 Bxf6 $15 { but now White does not have useful moves. } ) 
Bxf6 16. Qd2 a5 17. Rfc1 Nb4 $5 18. a3 ( { Or } 18. Nxb4 Qxb4 19. Qxb4 axb4 { threatening ...d4-d3. } 20. Ne1 Bg4 { when the e2-pawn is lost and Black has a pleasant edge. } ) 
Nxd3 19. exd3 Bf5 { The pawn structure has been transformed, and the rest of the game bears no relevance to this chapter. What is important is that Black had a pleasent advantage and won very convincingly. This short fragment teaches us how difficult it is to play against an isolated queens pawn once it has reached the fifth rank. } 
